1.6 Background Job Control — System Worker Pool
This page is the DevOps/Admin operational control center for all background jobs running inside Kincore.
It should be part of the Platform Admin Portal / DevOps Panel, not the Family Admin Panel. Family Admins should not access this page. Based on the PRD permission matrix, only Super Admin and DevOps Admin should be able to access Background Job Control.
A background job is a system task that runs behind the scenes without blocking the user experience. These jobs are required because Kincore has many heavy, scheduled, recurring, or reliability-sensitive processes such as PDF generation, media processing, storage recalculation, subscription sync, KCC Coin reconciliation, webhook retry, audit log archival, and database backup.
This page should allow DevOps to monitor, control, retry, pause, resume, schedule, and audit those background processes.

1. Active Worker Pool
The Active Worker Pool badge shows whether the background job system is currently healthy.
Possible statuses should include:
Active — workers are online and processing jobs normally.
Degraded — some jobs are delayed, some workers are unhealthy, or queue backlog is increasing.
Paused — job processing has been intentionally paused.
Offline — no worker is currently available.
Overloaded — queue backlog, runtime, or failure rate is above threshold.
This status should be calculated based on:
Worker heartbeat
Queue backlog
Failed job rate
Average runtime
Last processed timestamp
Dead-letter queue count
This aligns with the PRD requirement for background job monitoring, queue backlog monitoring, and platform reliability operations.

2. Schedule Job Button
The Schedule Job button is used by DevOps to create or update system-level scheduled jobs.
It should support:
Job name
Job type
Description
Schedule type
Cron expression
Run once / recurring / event-triggered
Worker group
Priority
Retry policy
Timeout limit
Concurrency limit
Environment: production / staging / testing
Enabled / disabled status
Example scheduled jobs:
Daily database backup
Storage usage recalculation
Subscription status sync
Audit log archival
Temporary PDF cleanup
Story expiration cleanup
KCC Coin reconciliation
Webhook retry processing
Dead-letter queue recovery
For production jobs, schedule changes must require a reason, confirmation, and audit log.

3. Search and Filter
The search bar should allow DevOps to search jobs by:
Job ID
Job name
Worker group
Description
Related user ID
Related family space ID
Related request ID
Related transaction ID
Last error message
The status filter should support:
Running
Success
Failed
Retrying
Pending
Scheduled
Paused
Cancelled
Timeout
Stuck
Dead-lettered
This helps DevOps quickly locate failed, delayed, or risky background processes.

4. Job Rows in the Table
Each row represents one system job.
JOB-BACKUP — Daily DB Backup
This job handles automated database backups.
Functionality:
Run daily backup
Verify backup completion
Store backup metadata
Track backup status
Alert DevOps if backup fails
Support restore-plan readiness
This directly matches the PRD requirement for automated daily backups and restore plan.

JOB-THUMBNAIL — Media Thumbnail Compression
This job processes uploaded media.
Functionality:
Compress uploaded photos or videos
Generate thumbnails
Optimize preview images
Reduce media loading time
Update media records after processing
Example:
User uploads an ancestor photo. The original file is stored in Supabase Storage or equivalent S3-compatible storage. The thumbnail job creates a smaller preview image so the app loads faster.
This aligns with the PRD media repository requirement for upload, compression, thumbnail generation, and S3-compatible storage.

JOB-STORAGE — Storage Usage Recalculation
This job recalculates storage usage by Family Space.
Functionality:
Count uploaded media, documents, generated PDFs, and other stored files
Update Family Space storage usage
Compare usage against subscription quota
Flag over-quota Family Spaces
Support billing and plan enforcement
Important clarification:
This is not the same as DevOps infrastructure resource usage.
 This job calculates Family Space business-level storage usage.

JOB-SUBSCRIPTION — Subscription Status Sync
This job syncs billing and subscription status.
Functionality:
Check active subscriptions
Detect expired or failed payments
Apply plan changes
Update Family Space entitlements
Update storage quota and feature access
Support backend feature gating
This aligns with the PRD pricing and monetization requirement that subscription tiers, feature flags, and backend gating must be enforced by backend logic.

JOB-ABUSE — Abuse Report Aggregation
This job aggregates abuse reports and trust/safety signals.
Functionality:
Group repeated reports
Detect high-risk Family Spaces
Escalate serious cases
Feed cases into the Trust & Safety workflow
Support L1/L2/L3 moderation workflow
Support risk scoring
This aligns with the PRD’s Trust, Safety & Abuse Workflow Engine and Family Space Risk Scoring module.

JOB-PDF — PDF Report Cleanup / PDF Queue Support
This job manages generated PDF reports.
Functionality:
Support descendant report generation queue
Remove expired temporary PDFs
Keep authorized downloadable reports
Respect privacy rules
Hide minors
Honor field-level visibility
Store generated files in object storage
Important:
PDF generation itself should run through the backend job queue. Cleanup is only one part of the PDF job lifecycle.
This directly matches the PRD requirement that descendant report PDFs are generated by backend job queue and stored in object storage.

JOB-AUDIT — Audit Log Archival
This job archives old audit logs.
Functionality:
Move older logs into archive storage
Preserve immutable audit history
Reduce main database load
Support audit export by authorized roles
Retain sensitive action records such as config changes, wallet actions, moderation actions, refunds, and admin operations
Important:
Audit logs should not be casually deleted. They should be archived according to retention rules.
This aligns with the PRD requirement that privileged actions must be audited and that audit logs must be immutable.

Additional PRD-required jobs to include
To avoid future conflict, the worker pool should also support these jobs:
JOB-KCC-RECONCILIATION — reconcile KCC Coin ledger or external BigK API status
JOB-WEBHOOK-RETRY — retry failed payment, coin, mall, or notification webhooks
JOB-DEADLETTER-RECOVERY — review and reprocess permanently failed jobs
JOB-STORY-EXPIRY — expire stories after 24/48/72 hours
JOB-NOTIFICATION — send in-app, email, and push notifications
JOB-MALL-SYNC — sync Mall orders, listings, fulfillment, or settlement status
JOB-XP-ACHIEVEMENT — calculate XP and level updates from verified actions
JOB-PUBLIC-SEARCH-INDEX — update opt-in public people search index

5. Worker Column
The worker column shows which worker group is responsible for the job.
The screenshot currently shows Main Pod. To avoid infrastructure conflict, we should not use “Pod” unless the system is actually using Kubernetes.
Recommended wording:
Main Worker
Backup Worker
Media Worker
Storage Worker
PDF Worker
Wallet Worker
Webhook Worker
Notification Worker
Audit Worker
Mall Worker
This is safer and consistent with the PRD’s Node.js + Express + Supabase/PostgreSQL architecture.

6. Status Column
The status column shows the latest job state.
Each job should record:
Current status
Last started time
Last completed time
Duration
Retry count
Failure reason
Trigger source
Related request ID
Related incident ID, if applicable
Example:
JOB-KCC-RECONCILIATION failed because BigK API returned timeout. The job moves to retrying. If retries fail, it enters dead-letter queue and alerts DevOps.
This is important because the PRD requires retry strategy, dead-letter queue, and reconciliation for KCC/payment-related integrations.

7. Performance Column
The performance column shows how well the job is running.
Recommended metrics:
Average duration
Last run duration
Success rate
Failure rate
Retry count
Queue waiting time
Timeout count
Records processed
Dead-letter count
Example:
If PDF generation normally takes 3 minutes but suddenly takes 30 minutes, DevOps can identify possible storage, database, or worker performance issues.

8. Runtime Column
The runtime column should show either:
Last run time, for completed jobs
Current running duration, for active jobs
Failed time, for failed jobs
Next scheduled run, for scheduled jobs
Example:
Running for 4m 12s
 Failed 10 minutes ago
 Next run: Today 2:00 AM

9. Actions Column
Each job should support controlled actions.
Recommended actions:
View Details
Shows:
Job definition
Worker group
Schedule
Retry policy
Timeout policy
Last execution result
Error message
Related logs
Related audit records
Run Now
Manually triggers the job.
Example:
DevOps manually runs storage recalculation after fixing a storage-counting issue.
Pause
Temporarily disables the job.
Example:
Pause PDF generation during storage migration.
Resume
Re-enables a paused job.
Retry
Retries a failed job.
Example:
Retry failed webhook delivery after external API recovers.
Cancel
Cancels a queued or running job, only if safe.
Critical jobs such as payment, wallet, subscription, and audit jobs should require stronger confirmation.
View Logs
Opens Logs Explorer filtered by job ID.
Example:
Click JOB-STORAGE → View Logs → Logs Explorer opens with job_id=JOB-STORAGE.
Edit Schedule
Allows DevOps to update job schedule.
This must be audited.

10. Permission and Audit Rules
This page must follow the PRD’s Platform Admin Portal access model.
Allowed roles:
Super Admin
DevOps Admin
Not allowed:
Family Admin
Branch Admin
Editor
Member
Guest/Public
Finance Admin, unless only viewing billing-related job results
Moderation Admin, unless only viewing abuse-related job results
Every manual action must be recorded in AuditLog:
Actor
Role
Action
Job ID
Environment
Old value
New value
Reason
Timestamp
Status
Related incident ID, if any
Examples:
DevOps Admin manually retried JOB-WEBHOOK-RETRY.
 DevOps Admin paused JOB-PDF.
 Super Admin changed JOB-BACKUP schedule from 12:00 AM to 2:00 AM.

11. Important PRD Conflict Avoidance
To ensure no conflict with the PRD, please follow these rules:
This page must remain inside Platform Admin / DevOps Panel only.
 It must not be exposed to Family Admin Panel.
Do not allow this page to mint, burn, or override KCC Coin ledger entries.
 Kincore may monitor KCC activity, flag suspicious activity, trigger freeze via BigK API, and view reconciliation status, but treasury and tokenomics control remain in BigK Admin.
Do not treat job actions as silent backend overrides.
 All production job actions must be audited.
Do not use AWS/Kubernetes-specific wording unless actually implemented.
 Use neutral terms like Worker Group, Worker Service, and Object Storage.
Do not delete audit logs through cleanup jobs.
 Audit logs should be archived, not casually deleted.
All payment, KCC, subscription, and webhook jobs must be idempotent.
 Retrying the same job must not duplicate coin credit, payments, refunds, notifications, or subscription changes.
All job failures that affect users should connect to Incident Management.
 For example, failed subscription sync, failed KCC reconciliation, or failed PDF generation at scale should be escalatable into an incident.

The System Worker Pool should be implemented as a full background job operations module under the Platform Admin / DevOps Panel.
It should manage all asynchronous, scheduled, recurring, delayed, retryable, and reliability-sensitive system tasks required by the PRD, including:
Backups
Media processing
Storage recalculation
Subscription sync
PDF generation and cleanup
Audit archival
Abuse aggregation
Story expiry
Webhook retry
Dead-letter recovery
KCC Coin reconciliation
Mall sync
Notifications
Public search indexing
XP/achievement calculation
The key principle is:
Any heavy, recurring, delayed, or failure-prone system process must run through the background job system, and DevOps must be able to monitor, retry, pause, resume, schedule, audit, and investigate it from this page.
This is aligned with the PRD and does not conflict as long as access control, audit logging, KCC governance limits, and Platform Admin separation are respected.
